---
/**
 * Home — the two-act landing. PUB-01, PUB-13, SEO-01. Plan 05-11.
 *
 * This route replaces the Phase 2 scaffold, and with it `src/components/StackProof.tsx`.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE MARKER. `stack-proof-ok` WAS A BEHAVIOURAL CONTRACT AND IT HAS BEEN MIGRATED, NOT REMOVED.
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * `GET /` returning a known marker is asserted by two suites and described in a third file:
 *
 *     test/harness/preview-reachable.node.test.ts   the built site is reachable over real workerd
 *     test/auth/deny-unauthenticated.node.test.ts   the AUTH-01/AUTH-04 suite — the middleware did
 *                                                   NOT run at build time and bake a 401 into the
 *                                                   public site's static HTML (threat T-02-35)
 *     src/middleware.ts                             states the contract in its own header
 *
 * The marker is now **`home-render-ok`**, carried by `data-home-marker` on this page's `<h1>`, and
 * all three sites moved in the same commit as the deletion.
 *
 * It is on the `<h1>` on purpose, and the `<h1>` is a design-system `Heading` — a React 19
 * component rendered with NO `client:*` directive. That preserves everything the old marker
 * proved, in one assertion: the build ran, a React component became static HTML with no hydration
 * script, the output was served by workerd, and the middleware let the prerendered route through.
 * A marker on a hand-written element would have proved less.
 *
 * It is an ATTRIBUTE rather than the heading's text. The heading's text is
 * `home_config.json`'s `title`, which is reviewed content an editor may legitimately change; an
 * attribute in code cannot be edited out of the CMS by accident.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * ACT 1 IS THE LEGACY COMPOSITION, REBUILT — AND THE DESIGN SOURCES OUTRANK 05-UI-SPEC.md
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * Phase 5 built this page from `05-UI-SPEC.md`, which had drifted from the design handoff nobody
 * diffed it against. The result was technically immaculate and visually flat: left-aligned at
 * 1080px, a sans 13px intro, six separately-rounded tiles and a dead band above the fold. Akhil
 * compared it with the site it replaced and asked for the original back.
 *
 * There are two authoritative sources, both in the repository, and where they disagree the rule
 * applied here is **legacy for LAYOUT, handoff for DETAIL** — every choice is named in this
 * plan's report:
 *
 *   1. `git show legacy/nextjs-portfolio:src/styles/home.css` + `:src/app/page.tsx`
 *      the site Akhil actually liked. `.home-d` is `max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;
 *      min-height: 100vh; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center;
 *      justify-content: center` — everything CENTRED, horizontally and vertically, in one
 *      viewport. That is the whole complaint about what shipped, and §1 of `home.css` is it.
 *   2. `design_handoff_portfolio/README.md` + `Akhil Saxena - Home.dc.html`
 *      the approved high-fidelity design. Every value specified; the 60px name, the serif voice
 *      on all three identity lines, the `ALL n →` badge and the mono scroll cue come from here.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE 60px NAME — AN UPSTREAM FINDING, AND THE FIX HAS TO SURVIVE A MEDIA QUERY
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * §3.1 and the handoff both ask for Playfair `--text-5xl` (60px) / 700 / `--lh-tight`.
 *
 * MEASURED, `dist/components/Heading.d.ts`: `HeadingSizeToken` is `"2xs" … "4xl"` and STOPS at
 * `4xl` (44px). The scale itself ships `--text-4xl-plus` (52) and `--text-5xl` (60) — both
 * confirmed in `dist/tokens.css` — so the component's token union does not expose two steps its
 * own type scale defines. **This is D-7, filed by 05-09 and still open in beta.2** — not a new
 * finding; the escape hatch below is.
 *
 * Consequence, MEASURED in `chunk-DQHLFJNO.js`: the numeric `size` path inlines `fontSize`,
 * `lineHeight: 1` and a size-derived `letterSpacing`, while the token path emits `data-size` and
 * inlines nothing. So a bare `<Heading className="hm-name">` inlines `font-size: 28px`, which no
 * class rule can beat, and a token size emits `.ds-atom-heading[data-size=…]` at (0,2,0) against
 * a class's (0,1,0), which no class rule can beat either.
 *
 * 🔴 AND THE NAME NOW HAS TO CHANGE SIZE ON A BREAKPOINT, WHICH THE OLD SPELLING COULD NOT DO.
 * The legacy design steps the name down at its narrow rung (`.hd-name` 3rem → 2.25rem at 600px)
 * and it must: at 390 the content box is 342px and "Akhil Saxena" at 60px measures ~360px, so a
 * fixed inline 60 either wraps the name or overflows the viewport. An inline style cannot be
 * changed by a media query.
 *
 * The escape hatch is the component's own: `style` spreads LAST in `Heading` (MEASURED, same
 * chunk), so `style={{ fontSize: 'var(--hm-name)' }}` beats the numeric path's inline `fontSize`,
 * and `--hm-name` is a custom property `home.css` §1 drives from the ladder's own 673px rung —
 * `--text-4xl` below it, `--text-5xl` at and above. Both are tokens; neither is a literal, and
 * `size={60}` is kept because its OTHER inline — the `--ls-tighter` letter-spacing derivation
 * above 28px — is exactly what §3.1 asks for and is not overridden.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE SERIF VOICE — A NEW UPSTREAM FINDING (D-22), TAKEN THE SAME WAY
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * All three identity lines are Playfair in BOTH sources. Phase 5 shipped the intro as sans 13px
 * (`Text` default), which is the single most-visible drift.
 *
 * MEASURED, `chunk-FNB2IYRN.js`: `Text`'s `baseStyle` inlines `fontFamily: "var(--font)"`, and
 * the component's only family lever is `mono` — a BOOLEAN that swaps to `var(--mono)`. There is
 * no `display`/`serif` selector, so on a site whose voice IS the serif, body text in the brand
 * face cannot be expressed through the component's API at all, and no consumer class can beat an
 * inline style. **Filed as D-22.**
 *
 * Until it lands, the same documented escape hatch: `style` spreads last, so the family is handed
 * through as `var(--display)` at the call site. It is a token, not a literal — this is the design
 * system speaking through the page, which is what `gate:app-css` calls a handover.
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * THE CTAs COME FROM `home_config.json` AND ARE `Link`s, NOT BUTTONS — D-5, D-6 AND D-4
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * `home_config.ctas` is CMS content Akhil edits: two records, `{ text, link, style }`, and the
 * legacy page renders both (`.hd-cta-primary` filled, `.hd-cta-secondary` outlined). The record
 * is READ here and never written.
 *
 * The legacy treatment is two BUTTON-SHAPED ANCHORS and the design system cannot express one:
 * MEASURED, `Button` extends `ButtonHTMLAttributes<HTMLButtonElement>` and has no `as` prop, so
 * it is always a `<button>`; `Link` HAS `as?: ElementType` but its variants are text links, and
 * the one that fits semantically — `action`, "bold action link with trailing arrow" — inlines
 * `textDecorationColor: "rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25)"`, a literal colour that is invisible on `#0d0d0f`
 * and is the same root cause as the `Footer` link defect already filed.
 *
 * The Core Value says a gap the design system exposes is a FINDING, not a workaround, so the
 * bespoke filled button is not re-implemented in app CSS here. **BOTH HALVES WERE ALREADY FILED**
 * — `Button`'s missing `as` is D-5 (and D-6, which files the same asymmetry across four components)
 * and `action`'s inline colour is D-4 — so this plan adds no number and cites theirs. The CTAs
 * ship as `Link variant="default"` / `"quiet"`, the two stylesheet-only variants. That is also
 * the house precedent: `/resume`'s primary CTA, "Download the PDF", is exactly this spelling.
 * **This is the one place the design system beat the legacy design, and it is reported as such.**
 *
 * ================================================================================================
 * ZERO FRAMEWORK JAVASCRIPT (PUB-14, §5.1 route 1)
 * ================================================================================================
 *
 * No `client:*` directive appears on this page or in any component it renders. Every
 * design-system component below is server-rendered to static HTML. The two acts are `min-height`,
 * DOM order, a real `<a href="#work">` and a `position: sticky` — there is nothing to hydrate,
 * which is also why there is nothing to trap a keyboard user in.
 */
import { Heading } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Heading';
import { Link } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Link';
import { Text } from '@akhil-saxena/design-system/components/Text';
import homeConfig from '../../data/home_config.json';
import HomeActTwo from '../components/public/HomeActTwo.astro';
import PeekGrid from '../components/public/PeekGrid.astro';
import Seo from '../components/public/Seo.astro';
import PublicLayout from '../layouts/PublicLayout.astro';
import '../styles/home.css';

/**
 * §13.2, the copy contract's Home row, verbatim including the arrow — and the design handoff's
 * scroll cue, whose own wording is `↓ THE WORK`.
 *
 * THE PINNED STRING WINS OVER THE HANDOFF'S WORDING HERE, deliberately: it is asserted character
 * for character by `test/public/home.node.test.ts`, it says what the control DOES rather than
 * where it points, and changing it would be a navigation change for no visual gain — the cue's
 * appearance (mono, `--text-xs`, wide tracking, centred under the CTAs) is the handoff's and is
 * carried by `home.css` §3. Recorded as a handoff/legacy divergence in this plan's report.
 */
const SCROLL_PROMPT = 'SCROLL FOR THE WORK ↓';

/**
 * The behavioural marker `GET /` must carry. See this file's header, and `src/middleware.ts`,
 * which names the same string.
 */
const HOME_RENDER_MARKER = 'home-render-ok';

/**
 * The identity lines and the two CTAs, all four READ from `data/home_config.json` and none of
 * them written. Home's three strings are deliberately unpinned so Akhil can edit them through
 * `/admin`; `test/public/copy-contract.node.test.ts` asserts the page renders what the RECORD
 * says rather than a literal, so editing the record moves both sides together.
 */
const { title, subtitle, intro, ctas } = homeConfig;

/**
 * The `style` field on a CTA record is CONTENT, so it cannot be trusted to name a design-system
 * variant directly — a typo in the CMS would render an unstyled link with no error. It is mapped,
 * and an unmapped value THROWS at build time rather than shipping a bare anchor on the site's
 * primary route.
 *
 * `default` and `quiet` are the only two stylesheet-only `Link` variants. §4.6a MEASURED that
 * `inline`, `footer` and `action` set `color` — and `action` and `footer` also set
 * `textDecorationColor: "rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.25)"` — as INLINE styles, which beat any app rule at any
 * specificity while every jsdom test still passes, because jsdom implements no CSS specificity.
 * On `#0d0d0f` that underline is invisible. Do not "improve" these to `action`; see D-4.
 */
const CTA_VARIANT: Record<string, 'default' | 'quiet'> = {
  primary: 'default',
  secondary: 'quiet',
};

const homeCtas = ctas.map((cta) => {
  const variant = CTA_VARIANT[cta.style];
  if (!variant) {
    throw new Error(
      `Home: home_config.ctas has style "${cta.style}", which has no Link variant in CTA_VARIANT ` +
        `(${Object.keys(CTA_VARIANT).join(', ')}). Rendering an unstyled anchor would put an ` +
        "unthemed control in the middle of the site's first viewport with nothing to catch it.",
    );
  }
  return { ...cta, variant };
});
---

<PublicLayout mainClass="pub-max-home">
  <Seo slot="head" title={`${title} — ${subtitle}`} description={intro} canonical="/" />

  {
    /*
    State A is a plain <div>, not a <section>: an unnamed <section> is not exposed as a region, and
    a named one here would make FOUR landmarks where §6.6.4 specifies three. The gallery inside it
    carries `aria-label="Photographs"`.

    Nothing in either state is hidden from assistive technology. State A moving out of view is a
    SCROLL POSITION — `display: none`, `visibility: hidden`, `aria-hidden` and `inert` are all
    forbidden on both states, and a screen-reader user reads photographs → work → résumé linearly
    and never encounters the two-state framing at all.

    `.hm-stage` INSIDE `.hm-a` is the legacy `.home-d` column, and the split is load-bearing:
    `.hm-a` owns the VIEWPORT BUDGET (`min-height`, `position: sticky`, the full 1080px page
    measure it inherits) while `.hm-stage` owns the 800px CENTRED COLUMN the composition is.
    Written as one element, the sticky box would be 800px wide and Act 2 would scroll over a
    strip rather than over the act. See `home.css` §1.
  */
  }
  <div class="hm-a">
    <div class="hm-stage">
      <header class="hm-identity">
        <Heading
          level={1}
          size={60}
          weight={700}
          style={{ fontSize: 'var(--hm-name)', lineHeight: 'var(--lh-tight)' }}
          data-home-marker={HOME_RENDER_MARKER}
        >
          {title}
        </Heading>

        {
          /* Playfair italic, muted — the handoff's subtitle role and the legacy `.hd-tagline`.
            `style` spreads last in `Text`, which is the only way past its inline `font-family`;
            see this file's header and D-22. */
        }
        <Text
          as="p"
          className="hm-subtitle"
          size="lg"
          tone="muted"
          style={{ fontFamily: 'var(--display)', fontStyle: 'italic' }}
        >
          {subtitle}
        </Text>

        {
          /* SERIF, not sans. `.hd-intro` is `font-family: var(--font-serif)` in the legacy
            stylesheet and Playfair 21px in the handoff; Phase 5 shipped it sans at `--text-base`,
            which is the drift Akhil saw first. */
        }
        <Text
          as="p"
          className="hm-intro"
          size="lg"
          tone="secondary"
          style={{ fontFamily: 'var(--display)' }}
        >
          {intro}
        </Text>
      </header>

      <PeekGrid />

      {
        /* `.hd-ctas` — a centred row that becomes a stacked column at the narrow rung, exactly as
          the legacy stylesheet does it. The records are the CMS's; the variants are D-4's. */
      }
      <div class="hm-ctas">
        {
          homeCtas.map((cta) => (
            <Link href={cta.link} variant={cta.variant} className="hm-cta">
              {cta.text}
            </Link>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <a class="hm-prompt" href="#work">{SCROLL_PROMPT}</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <HomeActTwo />
</PublicLayout>
